  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and cool conditions continue through the \nweekend.\n\nLarge high pressure continues to be the primary driver of the \npleasant days and cool nights through the weekend. Expecting \nanother night of radiational cooling with calming winds and \nclear skies to significantly drop temperatures tonight and again\nSunday night... overnight lows away from the coast likely drop \ninto the mid 20s tonight and low to mid 30s Sunday night. 925mb \ntemperatures warm significantly over the next 24 to 36 hours, up\nfrom +3C this morning t0 +10C by Sunday evening. This will jump\nstart a warming trend for the week, with high temperatures \nSunday expected to be slightly higher compared to today... in \nthe upper 50s, possibly low 60s in the CT River Valley. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry, seasonably warm temperatures continue \nthrough much of the week. The next chance for rain likely \ndoesn't arrive until late Thursday or Friday. \n\nMid level ridging and high pressure dominates the pattern \nthrough Thursday, continuing the period of dry weather. \nAnomalously high heights and mid-level temperatures will \naccompany the pattern favoring above normal temperatures through\nmuch of the week. 925mb temperatures ranging between +12 to \n+15C will yield high temperatures in the 60s each day. Highs \naround 70 are possible in areas with the highest probabilities \nThursday as the mid-level thermal ridge axis shifts over. \n\nEnsemble guidance shows a consensus in a shift of the pattern \nlate Thursday into Friday as a mid-level trough pushes into the \nregion. This may be our next best shot at precipitation, \nsometime in the late Thursday-Saturday timeframe. However, there\nis still a considerable amount of spread across ensemble \nsolutions and deterministic guidance with respect to the track \nof this trough and its surface features. This will influence \ndetails such as precip probabilities, timing, and amounts. \nEnsemble guidance highlights probabilities 50-75% for 24hr \nprecipitation greater than 0.01\", dropping off to low \nprobabilities for greater than 0.5\". The ECMWF ensemble has \nleaned wetter with probabilities closer to 60-80% for 0.01\" or \ngreater and moderate probabilities for greater than 0.1\". \nOverall, there is potential for this to be our next chance for \nrain after a prolonged dry period although confidence is lower \nin the coverage, timing, and amounts of the rain at this point.",
